Labour's next leader will be chosen by  its half a million or so members in the  coming months Sky News has the result of  the very first poll of labour members  asking who they want to succeed Jeremy  Corbyn on behalf of the party members  project for the economic Social Research  Council run out of Queen Mary and Sussex  University's YouGov polled 1059 labour  members here's who they put down as  their first preference Shadow brexit  secretary Kirsten  comes out on top getting 31% of first  preferences korbinite favorite and  shadow business secretary Rebecca long  Bailey comes out second on 20% and the  rest trail those two when it comes to  voting labour runs a preferential system  meaning that members are asked to rank  who they want in numerical order if no  one gets over 50% then one candidate is  knocked out in each round and the votes  of the eliminated candidate  redistributed amongst the others so  using this method who does that suggest  the poll thinks will win in the opening  round  Leeson andis knocked out then in round 2  Emily Thornbury then in round 3 clive  Lewis and in round 4 Yvette Cooper Jess  Phillips is out in round five that  leaves just two contenders going  head-to-head in the final round here  Kirsten has a 22 point lead over his  rival giving him a comfortable victory  if this poll was replicated on Election  Day  kiss stahma beats Rebecca long Bailey in  every age category in every region  amongst men and women and amongst them  professional classes and the working  class is the only group that who favor  long Bailey over stommer  our labour members who voted to leave  the EU in 2016 remain as back stommer  now there are plenty of caveats when it  comes to working out Jeremy Corbyn  successor a pole isn't a prediction some  candidates may yet into the race or pull  out and it might not include some of the  other categories of supporter who turned  out to be eligible to vote but on the  basis of this early Peck polling  exercise it seems Jeremy Corbyn and his  team won the hearts of party members in  2015 but not the right to determine its  future direction in 2019
 

